Let the Harmony host capture and restore a User-mode Linux kernel

While the kernel waits for a /dev/harmony answer, the host may instead send
a control message with an image file descriptor. A capture writes the host
process's private memory, the data extents of guest physical memory, the
thread pointer and an inventory of open host descriptors into that file. It
runs on the initial host thread with signals blocked, so it neither flushes
nor drains anything and virtual time does not move.

A restore checks the image against the running process first and refuses
it when the layout or the descriptor inventory differ. It then kills the
stub processes, removes the epoll registrations, copies the image back,
including the host mappings of kernel virtual memory where vmalloc stacks
live, and returns to the point where the capture was taken. That point
rebuilds the stubs and the epoll registrations from the restored kernel
state and acknowledges the restore. The harmony_restore parameter asks the host for an image at
the end of boot, which restores a checkpoint into a fresh process.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried as a kernel diff under
consonance/harmony-linux/linux/patches/ per the repository's kernel-patch
exception.

